/* book exam traps: A humidifier and a nebulizer are not the same: a humidifier adds vapour and never a drug; a nebulizer makes droplets and can carry a drug. | Bigger droplets do not go deeper. Above about 5 µm, droplets settle in the upper airway and mouth. | The 44 mg/L figure is for saturated gas at 37 °C, not for room-temperature water or room air. | In the water-rate sum, match minutes with minutes or hours with hours. Multiplying mg/min by 60 gives mg/h, not g/h until you divide by 1000. | An HME is not a heated humidifier. It recycles exhaled heat and water and has no water chamber. */
