/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A short-wave diathermy unit takes 50 Hz mains, rectifies it, and feeds an oscillator.
   - Tissue heats by two losses.
   - The body is not heated by a travelling beam at this frequency.
   - Microwave diathermy uses 2450 MHz.
   - Ultrasonic diathermy uses a piezoelectric transducer (a crystal that vibrates when driven by a voltage) at 0.8–1 MHz.
   - All three methods raise tissue temperature.
   - Dose is not just a number on the dial.
   - Contraindications include metal implants in the field, pregnancy over the abdomen or pelvis, ischaemic tissue (poor blood supply), bleeding tissue and impaired sensation.
   - Surgical diathermy, or electrosurgery, uses radio-frequency current for cutting and coagulation (clotting).
*/
