/* book Worked example 2: Q: An ultrasound therapy head has an effective radiating area of 5 cm² and works at 1 MHz. It is set to 1.5 W/cm² continuous for 5 minutes. (a) Find the output power and the energy delivered. (b) Repeat for pulsed mode at a 20 % duty cycle with the same setting. (c) Find the wavelength in soft tissue, where sound travels at 1540 m/s.
   1. Continuous power P = intensity × ERA = 1.5 W/cm² × 5 cm² = 7.5 W.
   2. Time t = 5 × 60 = 300 s, so energy E = P t = 7.5 W × 300 s = 2250 J.
   3. Pulsed at 20 %: average intensity = 0.2 × 1.5 = 0.3 W/cm², average power = 0.3 × 5 = 1.5 W and energy = 1.5 × 300 = 450 J, one fifth of the continuous dose.
   4. Wavelength in tissue λ = v ÷ f = 1540 m/s ÷ (1 × 10⁶ Hz) = 1.54 × 10⁻³ m = 1.54 mm.
   5. Compare: the short-wave field has an 11 m wavelength, the ultrasound beam 1.54 mm. The short wavelength is why ultrasound forms a narrow beam that can be aimed, and why bone edges reflect it.
   Answer: Continuous: 7.5 W and 2250 J; pulsed at 20 %: 1.5 W average and 450 J; wavelength in tissue 1.54 mm. */
