/* book exam traps: Do not write that higher frequency always means deeper heating. For electromagnetic diathermy it is the opposite: 2450 MHz microwaves heat only a few centimetres, while 27.12 MHz short waves heat deeper. | Do not call 27.12 MHz just a convenient short-wave radio band. It is a reserved industrial–scientific–medical (ISM) frequency; 13.56 MHz is another. | Do not treat diathermy and surgical diathermy as the same. Therapeutic diathermy warms tissue; electrosurgery cuts and coagulates with concentrated RF current. | Do not say the dose is set by power alone. It is power, or for ultrasound intensity in W/cm², applied for a time, and it also depends on coupling, the applicator and the patient's sensation. | Do not write that 3 MHz ultrasound penetrates deeper than 1 MHz. Absorption rises with frequency, so 3 MHz heats more superficially. */
