/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Tissue is an electrolyte.
   - Four numbers describe the pulse.
   - Q is charge per pulse in coulombs (C); I is the set current in amperes (A); t is the pulse width in seconds (s).
   - A fifth number is the duty cycle: the fraction of time the current is on.
   - D is duty cycle (a fraction, no unit); t is pulse width in seconds (s); f is pulse frequency in hertz (Hz).
   - Electrodes are the other half of the circuit.
   - TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) puts electrodes over the painful area or the nerve supplying it.
   - EMS (electrical muscle stimulation) and NMES (neuromuscular electrical stimulation) act on motor nerves: the nerve fires and the muscle contracts.
   - Interferential therapy (IFT) uses two currents at about 4 kHz that differ by 1–150 Hz.
   - Older names are still asked.
*/
