/* book Worked example: Q: A TENS unit is set to amplitude 20 mA, pulse width 200 µs and frequency 100 Hz. The pulse is rectangular and monophasic for this calculation. Find (a) charge per pulse, (b) charge delivered per second, (c) duty cycle, (d) average current and (e) the charge density under one 5 cm × 5 cm electrode. Give units.
   1. Convert to SI: I = 20 mA = 0.020 A. t = 200 µs = 2×10⁻⁴ s. f = 100 Hz.
   2. Charge per pulse: Q = I × t = 0.020 × 2×10⁻⁴ = 4×10⁻⁶ C = 4 µC.
   3. Pulses per second = 100. Charge per second = 4×10⁻⁶ × 100 = 4×10⁻⁴ C/s = 0.4 mC/s.
   4. Duty cycle: D = t × f = 2×10⁻⁴ × 100 = 0.02 = 2 %.
   5. Average current: I × D = 0.020 × 0.02 = 4×10⁻⁴ A = 0.4 mA, which equals charge per second.
   6. Charge density: electrode area = 5 cm × 5 cm = 25 cm², so 4 µC ÷ 25 cm² = 0.16 µC/cm² per pulse. A 1 cm² electrode carrying the same pulse would put 4 µC/cm² under it, 25 times more.
   7. The nerve sees 20 mA for 200 µs; the long-term average is only 0.4 mA.
   Answer: Charge per pulse = 4 µC, charge per second = 0.4 mC/s, duty cycle = 2 %, average current = 0.4 mA, charge density = 0.16 µC/cm². */
